  "question": {
    "text": "A metal plate of area <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#160;</mo><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></math> is illuminated by a radiation of intensity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>16</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mfrac></math> . The work function of the metal is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>.</mo></math> The energy of the incident photons is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>10</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi></math> and only <math><mn>10</mn><mi>%</mi></math> of it produces photo electrons.&nbsp;The number of emitted photo electron&nbsp; per second and their maximum energy, respectively, will be: <math><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\" separators=\"|\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mtext> </mtext><mi>e</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><msup><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>19</mn></mrow></msup><mi>J</mi></mrow></mfenced></math>",
